course: world
lesson: 117
title: Other calendars: Chinese New Year, Islamic and Jewish calendars, and Christmas in Ukraine
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A calendar can follow the Sun, the Moon, or both. The Sun gives the year and its seasons; the Moon gives a month of about 29 and a half days, from one new moon to the next. Twelve Moon months make about 354 days, about 11 days short of a Sun year, so every calendar has to choose.
explain: The Islamic calendar follows only the Moon, so Ramadan, the month of fasting, comes about 11 days earlier each year and travels through every season in about 33 years: one summer with long fasting days, years later a winter with short ones.
explain: The Chinese and Jewish calendars follow both. Their months follow the Moon, but in some years they add a whole extra month, like a runner stopping to let a friend catch up, so Chinese New Year always lands between late January and February, and Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year, in September or October.
explain: Ukraine shows how calendars live inside families. The Orthodox church kept the old Julian calendar, so Christmas was on 7 January, which is 25 December in the Julian calendar. In 2023 the state Christmas holiday became 25 December, though some families and churches still keep 7 January, and many still enjoy the "Old New Year" on 14 January.
explain: New Year can be in winter, spring or autumn: Nowruz, the Persian New Year, comes on the first day of spring, about 20 or 21 March, and Ethiopian New Year in September.
fact: Chinese New Year starts at a new moon between 21 January and 20 February; in 2026 it was on 17 February.
fact: The Chinese zodiac has 12 animals in order: Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog, Pig; the cycle repeats every 12 years.
fact: 2016 was a Year of the Monkey, 2024 a Year of the Dragon and 2026 a Year of the Horse.
fact: The Islamic calendar follows only the Moon: 12 months of 29 or 30 days, about 354 days a year.
fact: Ramadan, when many Muslims fast from dawn to sunset, starts about 11 days earlier each year on our calendar.
fact: An Islamic month begins when the thin new crescent moon is seen, or by calculation, so Eid (the festival at the end of Ramadan) can fall a day apart in different places.
fact: Islamic years are counted from 622, when the Prophet Muhammad moved from Mecca to Medina; because the years are shorter, the year number is now about 1447 to 1448, more than 2026 − 622 = 1,404.
fact: In the Jewish calendar a day begins at sunset, so Shabbat, the day of rest, runs from Friday evening to Saturday night, about 25 hours.
fact: The Jewish calendar adds a 13th month in 7 years out of every 19, so its holidays always stay in the same season.
fact: 25 December in the old Julian calendar is 7 January in ours, because the gap is now 13 days.
fact: In 2023 Ukraine made 25 December the state Christmas holiday; some Ukrainians and churches still celebrate on 7 January.
fact: The "Old New Year" on 14 January is 1 January in the old Julian calendar.
fact: Diwali, the Hindu festival of lights, follows the Moon and the Sun and falls between mid-October and mid-November.
fact: Nowruz, the Persian New Year, is at the spring equinox, about 20 or 21 March, when day and night are about equal.
exercise: When does Chinese New Year fall on our calendar? | Between 21 January and 20 February | Always on 1 January, the same as ours | Always on 17 February, every single year | Between 21 June and 20 July, in summer
exercise: Mei was born in 2016. What is her Chinese zodiac animal? | Monkey | Dragon | Horse | Rat
exercise: How often does the Chinese zodiac repeat? | Every 12 years | Every 10 years | Every 7 years | Every 100 years
exercise: What does the Islamic calendar follow? | Only the Moon | Only the Sun | The Moon and the Sun | The stars of the zodiac
exercise: About how much earlier does Ramadan start each year on our calendar? | About 11 days | About 1 day | About 1 month | About 2 months
exercise: What do many Muslims do during Ramadan? | Fast from dawn to sunset | Eat only at noon each day | Fast for one single day | Eat only sweet food
exercise: What tells many Muslims that a new month, and Eid, has begun? | Seeing the thin new crescent moon | Seeing the round full moon rise up | The first snow of the winter falling | The longest and sunniest day of the year
exercise: From which event are Islamic years counted? | The Prophet Muhammad's move from Mecca to Medina in 622 | The building of the first mosque in 1 BC | The start of Caesar's calendar in 45 BC | The first Ramadan in the year 1000
exercise: Why is the Islamic year number, about 1447, bigger than 2026 − 622? | Its years are shorter, so more of them have passed | It started counting much earlier than the year 622 | It adds a leap month every year | It counts months instead of years
exercise: When does a day start in the Jewish calendar? | At sunset | At midnight | At sunrise | At noon
exercise: When does Shabbat begin? | On Friday evening at sunset | On Saturday morning at sunrise | On Sunday at midnight | On Friday morning at dawn
exercise: How does the Jewish calendar keep its holidays in the same season? | It adds a 13th month in some years | It follows only the Sun, never the Moon | It moves every holiday 11 days a year | It drops a month every year
exercise: Why did many Ukrainians celebrate Christmas on 7 January? | 7 January is 25 December in the old Julian calendar | The Bible says Jesus was born on 7 January | Shops in Ukraine were shut on 25 December | 7 January is the first day of spring there
exercise: Since 2023, what is the date of the state Christmas holiday in Ukraine? | 25 December | 7 January | 14 January | 19 December
exercise: What is the "Old New Year" on 14 January? | New Year's Day in the old Julian calendar | The very first day of the Chinese New Year | The last day of the holy fasting month of Ramadan | The day Ukraine celebrates its Independence Day
exercise: What is Diwali? | The Hindu festival of lights | The Jewish day of rest each week | The Persian New Year in the spring | The Muslim holy month of fasting
exercise: When is Nowruz, the Persian New Year? | At the spring equinox, about 20 or 21 March | On 1 January, exactly the same as in Britain | At the first new moon of the winter | On the longest day, about 21 June
test: Mei's mate: "Isn't New Year 1 Jan, end of?" Mei: "Not in our house, bestie." What's the best answer? | Different calendars have different New Years; Chinese New Year follows the Moon | Only 1 January is a real New Year; the others are pretend | Chinese New Year is on 1 January too, just with fireworks | Chinese New Year is only for people who live in China
test: Mei: "I'm a Dragon cos I'm a 2016 baby, no cap!" ("No cap" means honestly.) What's right? | She's a Monkey; the last Dragon year was 2024 | She's right, 2016 was a Dragon year | She's a Horse, like the year 2026 | She has no animal at all, as she was born abroad
test: What would happen to the season of Ramadan over about 33 years? | It would move through all four seasons and come back | It would stay in the very same month | It would happen twice every single year | It would move only one day later over all those years
test: Yusuf, in London: "Fasting in summer is long, innit, the days go on forever." Why would fasting in winter feel shorter? | Winter days are short, so dawn to sunset is fewer hours | In winter Ramadan has only 20 days | People do not fast at all in winter | The Sun sets right at noon in the middle of a British winter
test: Aisha's uncle in Bradford: "We wait for the moon, habibti." ("Habibti" means my dear, in Arabic.) Her cousin in another country had Eid a day earlier. How can that be? | Some places use moon sightings, others calculations, so dates can differ by a day | One of them must have read the wrong year | Eid lasts one hour, so places take turns | The Moon is new a week later in Bradford
test: Noa: "Shabbat starts Friday at sunset, 7:45 pm, and ends after dark on Saturday." About how long is it? | About 25 hours | About 12 hours | About 48 hours | About 7 hours
test: Dmytro's mate from Newcastle: "Howay man, did they shift Christmas or what?" ("Howay" means come on.) What's true? | Ukraine moved its state Christmas from the Julian date to 25 December in 2023 | Christmas moved because the Bible was rewritten | Nobody in Ukraine keeps 7 January any more | Christmas was always on 25 December in Ukraine
test: Dmytro's babusia (grandma): "Two New Years, mate, sorted!" ("Sorted" means great.) Why does she get two? | The Old New Year, 14 January, is 1 January in the Julian calendar | She counts the New Years of two different countries | The second one is really her birthday party | Ukraine has a New Year every six months
test: Priya: "Diwali's November this year and October last year, it's well random." Is it random? | No, it follows the Moon and Sun, so it moves but stays in autumn | Yes, a new date is drawn out of a hat each year | Yes, it can fall in any month at all | No, it is on the very same date every year
test: Arash, whose gran lives in Bristol: "Our New Year's the first day of spring, proper job!" ("Proper job" is West Country for great.) When is it? | About 20 or 21 March | Exactly 1 January, like us | About 21 June, midsummer | About 1 September each year
test: A video game shows Chinese New Year fireworks on 1 January. What's wrong? | Chinese New Year falls between 21 January and 20 February | Nothing, Chinese New Year is always on 1 January everywhere | Chinese New Year is in high summer | Chinese New Year has no fireworks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Islamic calendar? | It is purely lunar, its year being approximately 354 days | It is purely solar, its year being 365 days | It adds a thirteenth month in seven of every nineteen years | It is identical with the Julian calendar
exam: Ascertain the date in the Gregorian calendar corresponding at present to 25 December in the Julian calendar. | 7 January | 14 January | 25 December | 19 January
exam: Identify the principal reason for which Rosh Hashanah invariably occurs in September or October. | An additional month is inserted in certain years, keeping the calendar in step with the Sun | The festival is fixed by law upon the first of September | The Jewish calendar disregards the Moon entirely | The festival is observed only once in every nineteen years
exam: Given that the Chinese zodiac comprises twelve animals in recurring succession and that 2024 was a Year of the Dragon, determine the animal of the year 2036. | The Dragon | The Horse | The Monkey | The Rat
